<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n46790" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἠϊόεις</span></b> , <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">εσσα</span>,
			<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">εν</span>, (<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἤϊα</span> A) 
			Ep.
		 word of doubtful meaning, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b>
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἐπ᾽ ἠϊόεντι Σκαμάνδρῳ</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Il.
					5.36
				</span></i>
			; perh. connected by later poets with <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἠϊών</span>, hence 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἠ. Πάνορμος</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Q.S.
					1.283
				</span></i>
			; <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">κόλλουρος</span>
			<i>haunting the shore</i>, <i><span class="en">
				Marc.Sid.
				22
			</span></i>: but perh. also with <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἤϊα</span>
			(A), 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">χήνεσιν ἠϊόεν πεδίον κάτα
					βοσκομένοισιν</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Q.S.
					5.299
				</span></i>
			; derived from <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἴον</span> by <i><span class="en">
				<u>EM</u>
				423.14
			</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
